    "content": "Former Somali President Sheikh Sharif Sheikh Ahmed has publicly rejected the election timetable recently announced by the Independent National Electoral and Boundaries Commission (INEBC). In a statement, he warned that proceeding with the disputed schedule could result in parallel elections and further destabilize the country's political landscape. The rejection adds to growing concerns about the credibility of the electoral process.\n\nThe INEBC, tasked with organizing Somalia's elections, released the timetable earlier this week, outlining key dates for the 2021 parliamentary and presidential polls. However, the plan has been met with resistance from several political stakeholders, including former President Sharif, who argue that the commission lacks the necessary consensus and legitimacy to oversee the process. Sharif's stance reflects broader grievances among opposition groups that have long called for a more inclusive and transparent electoral framework.\n\nSheikh Sharif Sheikh Ahmed served as Somalia's president from 2009 to 2012 and remains an influential figure in the country's politics. His rejection of the timetable underscores the deep divisions between the federal government and opposition parties, which have been at odds over the composition of the electoral commission and the selection of delegates. These disputes have raised fears of a return to political instability and potential violence.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the developments are particularly significant. Many diaspora members have invested in Somalia's peace process and are eager to see stable governance and democratic progress. A disputed election could undermine these efforts, delay remittances and investments, and complicate diaspora participation in rebuilding the homeland. Moreover, parallel elections would likely exacerbate clan tensions, affecting families both in Somalia and abroad.\n\nThe international community, including the United Nations and key donor countries, has repeatedly urged Somali leaders to resolve their differences and hold timely, credible elections. The rejection by Sharif and other opposition figures may prompt renewed mediation efforts to prevent a political crisis. How the federal government and the INEBC respond to these objections will be crucial in determining whether Somalia can avoid a contested electoral outcome.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/former-somali-president-rejects-election-schedule-warns-of-political-crisis/426541/",
