    "original_title": "Brisk livestock sales help Middle Shabelle pastoralists to repay drought debts",
    "slug": "livestock-sales-in-middle-shabelle-enable-pastoralists-to-repay-drought-debts",
    "summary": "Recent rains have improved pasture and livestock conditions in Middle Shabelle, allowing pastoralists to sell animals and repay debts incurred during three years of drought.",
    "content": "Pastoralist families in the Warsheikh and Adale districts of Somalia's Middle Shabelle region have started to repay debts accumulated during a severe three-year drought. The repayments follow recent rains that have replenished pasture and water sources, leading to healthier livestock. As a result, livestock markets in Mogadishu have seen increased activity, with pastoralists bringing well-fed animals for sale.\n\nOne pastoralist, Abdullahi Muhyadin Ahmed from rural Adale, sold 50 goats for $4,200 in early August. He used $1,200 of the proceeds to repay local traders who had provided food and clothing for his family of nine during the drought. This pattern is being repeated across the region as pastoralists seek to settle debts and rebuild their livelihoods.\n\nThe drought, which persisted for three years, had devastated livestock herds and forced many families into debt to survive. The recent rains have brought relief, but the recovery is fragile. Access to markets and fair prices remains critical for pastoralists to fully regain their economic stability.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are significant. Many diaspora members send remittances to relatives in rural areas, and the ability of pastoralists to repay debts and become self-sufficient reduces the burden on these support networks. Additionally, the improved livestock condition may lead to increased exports of Somali livestock to Gulf countries, a key source of foreign currency for the nation.\n\nHowever, challenges persist. The region remains vulnerable to climate shocks, and sustained support is needed to ensure long-term resilience. The diaspora can play a role by continuing to invest in sustainable projects and supporting local economies.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/brisk-livestock-sales-help-middle-shabelle-pastoralists-to-repay-drought-debts/",
